    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAATH//UXo\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/common/base64_utils.py",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 64,
      "author": {
        "id": 8116
      },
      "writtenOn": "2013-10-25T16:17:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "(NIT) I guess that depends on your definition of a constant. Are module scoped objects created at run time constants? I generally don\u0027t think of these as constants because they\u0027re actually variables that can be assigned to and whose value is the result of executing code (function call). Maybe too many years of C coding has coloured my definition of a constant. Technically Python doesn\u0027t have any constants. If you mean a constant is any variable which you\u0027re not supposed to modify then yes, they\u0027re constants. If these were simple values (e.g. a string or an integer value) as opposed to the result of a function call I\u0027d be much more comfortable calling them a constant, but these are actually function call results.\n\nIf you still feel strongly these identifiers should be all caps I\u0027ll change it.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAATH//dNc\u003d",
      "revId": "917eb0665fa8e391fb58daf28438cab010d5a606",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
